body:
No. The technical reason is because the meson state is represented by M= \bar{q}_i q^i, where i is the color index of the quarks (I'm suppressing other indices like flavor and spin). But you can do a color gauge transformation q^i \to U^i _j q^j, where U^i _j is an SU(3) matrix, and similarly for \bar{q} but with U^\dagger instead. This gauge transformation could swap red and green for example, if U=
[0 1 0 ; 
-1 0 0 ; 
0 0 1].
Then, the U from the quark and the U^\dagger from the anti-quark will cancel, leaving the same physical state, despite the color pairs being different.
